    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for that. just to be super clear though, what's the main thing that causes people to get stopped at the border? like is it just missing a stamp/paper, or are there specific 'hidden' rules regarding the type of transit they're on that usually catch people off guard? i want to make sure they don't run into any unexpected trouble with the officials when they are just trying to get through the terminal.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In most cases, tourists and business travelers typically don't get stopped at the airport, except in situations where there's a discrepancy or suspicion about their visit.\n\n1. Transit visas are primarily meant for long term business trips from one country to another, and usually an entry visa is required to enter a foreign country. If a full business visa has not been applied for then you should not be able to board an aircraft to another country!\n2. Business travelers and residents who have just transited through the airports in Singapore, Malaysia or Thailand such as to use an ATM, to rest in a hotel, or to carry on board to their final destination.\n\nAirlines should provide transit visas, especially if the transit visa is required. Most travel insurance carriers offer travel documentation insurance, so if there was no policy that covered this, I suggest talking to your travel insurance provider professional to see if they offer any transit visa assistance or travel documentation plan that covers the companies travel. You will likely pay for this out-of-pocket.\n\nWhat people get stopped for:\n\n1. Leaving or staying unexpectedly longer on a transit visa that has a hard departure time attached to it, i.e., it was stipulated in the visa start time that customers have to leave by a certain time in order to be",
      "source": "target",
